.TH BFC-RUNTIME 7 "2026-09-25" "bfc" "Miscellaneous Information"
.SH NAME
bfc-runtime \- runtime calls available to compiled Brainfuck programs
.SH DESCRIPTION
In addition to the eight standard Brainfuck commands,
.B bfc
supports
.B !
as a runtime-call extension. The current tape cell contains the operation
number. Arguments follow in subsequent cells, and the operation result
replaces the current cell unless the operation exits the process. Strings are
NUL-terminated. File handles are byte values; handle zero is invalid for file
operations except where an operation explicitly uses it for standard input
or output.
.PP
The available operations are:
.TP
.B 1
Return the program argument count.
.TP
.B 2
Take a zero-based argument index in the next cell, copy that argument as a
NUL-terminated string starting two cells after the operation, and return its
length modulo 256.
.TP
.B 3
Take a mode and a NUL-terminated path. Mode 0 opens for reading and selects
the file for the
.B ,
command; mode 1 opens for writing; mode 2 opens for appending. Return a file
handle, or zero on failure.
.TP
.B 4
Take a file handle and read one byte, returning zero at end of file.
.TP
.B 5
Take a file handle and a byte to write. Return 1 on success.
.TP
.B 6
Take a file handle and close it. Return 1 on success.
.TP
.B 7
Select a file handle for the
.B ,
command. Handle zero selects standard input. Return 1 on success.
.TP
.B 8
Select a file handle for the
.B .
command. Handle zero selects standard output. Return 1 on success.
.TP
.B 9
Run a NUL-terminated command through the system shell. Return 1 on success.
.TP
.B 10
Create a temporary file, copy its path starting two cells after the operation,
and select it for the
.B .
command.
.TP
.B 11
Take an output path and link the most recent temporary LLVM module with Clang.
An empty path selects
.BR a.out .
Return zero on success and 1 on failure. This operation supports the compiler
bootstrap.
.TP
.B 12
Exit the process with the status in the next cell.
.TP
.B 13
Return zero when
.B BFC_BOOTSTRAP=1
and 1 otherwise. This operation supports the compiler bootstrap.
.TP
.B 14
Write a NUL-terminated message starting two cells after the operation to
standard error. Return 1 on success.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bfc (1)
